---
interface Props { top?: boolean }
// On its own page the case title is the h1 and each beat label an h2; inside
// the one-page version they sit one level lower. Beat labels are headings so
// screen-reader users can jump beat to beat.
const { top = false } = Astro.props;
const HT = top ? 'h1' : 'h2';
const HL = top ? 'h2' : 'h3';
const HD = top ? 'h3' : 'h4'; // decision titles
import Demo from './Demo.astro';
import FxHero from './FxHero.astro';
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import { imageWidths } from '../lib/image-widths';
import dataLayers from '../assets/img/fx-figma/data-layers.png';
import ProductCapture from './ProductCapture.astro';
import Split from './Split.astro';
import CaseContents from './CaseContents.astro';
import ResponsiveEvidence from './ResponsiveEvidence.astro';
import DecisionMap from './dg/DecisionMap.astro';
import FxOriginalFlow from './dg/FxOriginalFlow.astro';
import FxPersonas from './dg/FxPersonas.astro';
import FxDesignEvidence from './FxDesignEvidence.astro';
import FxJourney from './dg/FxJourney.astro';

import Flow from './dg/Flow.astro';
import Layers from './dg/Layers.astro';
import Matrix from './dg/Matrix.astro';
import BeforeAfter from './dg/BeforeAfter.astro';
---
<section id="fx" data-tone="dark" class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="chapter-head">
      <div class="g" style="row-gap:28px;align-items:end">
        <div style="grid-column:1/9;display:grid;gap:24px">
          <p class="eyebrow">Case one · Barclays · 2025 to now</p>
          <HT class="h-chapter" style="view-transition-name:t-fx">FX execution analytics</HT>
        </div>
        <p class="lead muted" style="grid-column:1/9">A trader works a large FX order over hours and keeps deciding whether to step in or leave it. The desk did that across a static chart, five spreadsheets and a separate execution tool.</p>
        <div class="meta" style="grid-column:1/13"><span><b>Role</b> · Lead designer, one of one</span><span><b>Team</b> · ~20 engineers, business, charting vendor</span><span><b>Status</b> · Beta with internal users</span><span><b>Platform</b> · Web, light and dark</span><span><b>Live</b> · <a href="https://shannonhecker.github.io/fx-execution-analytics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">My public rebuild<span class="vh"> (opens in new tab)</span> →</a></span></div>
      </div>
      <FxHero />
    </header>
    <CaseContents project="fx" />


    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-problem">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>01</b> Problem</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Keep the trader in one place, and cut the time from seeing a price move to acting on it." />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>Users</b><p>The sales and trading desk, and the institutional clients executing through the same platform with different entitlements.</p></li>
          <li><b>Where we started</b><p>One static Execution Over Time chart from the desk. Five spreadsheets covering trades, market data per order, price limit logs and reference prices. A twelve-item wishlist. No design, no interaction model, no visual language.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="project-status"><span class="project-status-label">Project status</span><p>In beta with internal users at Barclays. Under NDA, so the screens here are my public rebuild and Figma designs, using sample data and based on the production spec.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-users">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>02</b> Users and research</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="A trader who acts, a desk head who defends, and a quant who tunes." />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>Professionals whose job is the tool</b><p>A trader will tolerate density because power is worth the learning curve. So the problem is hierarchy under pressure, not simplification.</p></li>
          <li><b>Five spreadsheets, read line by line</b><p>Fills, market data, limit price changes, arrival price, risk transfer price. I grouped them into four kinds of data and that grouping became the layers of the chart. Every mark maps to a data type. Nothing is decorative.</p></li>
          <li><b>The limit price log was the clue</b><p>Orders were being changed mid-flight, several times each. So repricing is a core action, not an edge case, and it had to live on the chart.</p></li>
          <li><b>Watching people decide</b><p>I sat with a few clients to watch how they decide to trade now or wait. The question is always the same. Am I ahead of the market or behind it. That's the first thing the screen answers.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <figure class="fx-source-layers">
          <a href={dataLayers.src} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="View full size: FX data-to-encoding diagram (opens in new tab)"><Picture src={dataLayers} quality={85} widths={imageWidths(dataLayers.width)} sizes="90vw" formats={['avif','webp']} alt="Market data becomes continuous lines; limit-price changes become steps; states become ranges; fills become sized dots; benchmarks become fixed reference lines. Each shape gets its own encoding." loading="lazy" /></a>
          <figcaption>My data-to-encoding diagram, from the updated desktop design.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <details class="fx-layer-transcript"><summary>Explore each data layer</summary>
        <Layers
          label="Understanding the data: five spreadsheets, four kinds of data, one mark each"
          cols={[
            { from: ['Market data'], kind: 'Market context', note: 'The world around the order', enc: 'Ticks become lines', encNote: 'Streaming quotes drawn as continuous lines', glyph: 'line' },
            { from: ['Limit price log'], kind: 'Order intent', note: 'What the trader asked for', enc: 'Events become steps', encNote: 'Each amendment holds until the next', glyph: 'steps' },
            { from: ['Order states'], kind: 'Order intent', note: 'Working, suspended, outside limit', enc: 'States become ranges', encNote: 'Each state holds from start to end', glyph: 'range' },
            { from: ['Fills'], kind: 'Execution results', note: 'What the algo actually did', enc: 'Fills become sized dots', encNote: 'Dot size is volume, colour is venue', glyph: 'dots' },
            { from: ['Arrival price', 'Risk transfer price'], kind: 'Benchmarks', note: 'How the outcome is judged', enc: 'Constants become references', encNote: 'Fixed lines every fill is judged against', glyph: 'dash' },
          ]}
          result="Each kind of data gets its own shape. Nothing on the chart is decorative."
          caption="The same four branches as my Figma diagram, with selectable text and a stacked layout on smaller screens."
        />        </details>

        <FxPersonas />
      </div>
    </div>

    <FxJourney />

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-constraints">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>03</b> Constraints</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Four things shaped every decision. None of them were mine to remove." />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>One platform, two audiences, old technology</b><p>Internal traders and external clients on the same system with different entitlements. One design had to serve both without a second codebase.</p></li>
          <li><b>A charting vendor's engine</b><p>Some ideas were possible, some weren't, and some were possible but wrong. The vendor's limits became inputs, not blockers.</p></li>
          <li><b>Compliance in the flow</b><p>Every action that changes an order needs a confirmation step and an audit trail. Safety could not mean leaving the chart.</p></li>
          <li><b>The foundation was broken, so I fixed it first</b><p>The brief was to tidy the light theme. I read the code, the tokens and the naming and found hardcoded colours throughout, so one fix would break the next. I gave the product owner and engineering two options with costs. We shipped the feature work first, then phased the fix in the background, starting with the blotter because it was simple and single-themed.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-explore">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>04</b> Explorations</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Watch, assess, step in. Every branch lands on a screen." />
        <p class="lead muted">Two questions, one screen. How far along is my order, and am I getting a good price. Show them apart and the trader flicks between charts under pressure. Mash them together and it's noise. The working principle: readable in two seconds, actionable in one click.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-decisions">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>05</b> Decisions and trade-offs</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Three calls that defined the product. Each one had a real alternative." />
        <DecisionMap project="fx"><FxOriginalFlow slot="flow" /></DecisionMap>
        <ol class="dec">
          <li><span class="n">01</span><div><HD>Progress and price on one canvas, not two panels</HD><p>We explored two linked panels and one combined chart. Two panels were cleaner to build, but traders flicked between them under pressure and missed the moment.</p><p class="verdict">One canvas with a dual axis won, at the cost of a denser screen that needed careful hierarchy.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">02</span><div><HD>Reprice on the chart, not in a settings menu</HD><p>The limit price log showed orders being changed mid-flight. We tested a menu-based edit against drag-to-reprice on the price line.</p><p class="verdict">Drag won because the decision and the action sit in the same place, with a pre-filled ticket as the safety step.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="n">03</span><div><HD>Studies on market prices only. A decision I reversed</HD><p>My spec let analytics studies run on any series, including the order's own fills. The vendor showed that order data has gaps, so a study on it looks confident and is wrong.</p><p class="verdict">I reversed it. Studies run on bid, ask and mid only, and the picker says why. Flexibility that produces plausible but wrong output isn't a feature.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-flow">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>06</b> The full flow</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="From monitoring to action, without leaving the chart." />
    <section class="screen-sequence" aria-labelledby="fx-live-modes">
      <h3 id="fx-live-modes">One workflow, two presentations</h3>
      <p class="muted">Classic represents my Barclays design. These are captures of my public rebuild with simulated data, not private bank screens.</p>
      <ProductCapture name="live-fx-classic.png" alt="Classic FX public rebuild in dark mode, showing the complete order and execution chart" href="https://shannonhecker.github.io/fx-execution-analytics/" label="Public rebuild · Classic · Dark mode · Simulated data" />
      <ProductCapture name="live-fx-classic-light.png" alt="The same Classic FX workspace in light mode" href="https://shannonhecker.github.io/fx-execution-analytics/" label="Public rebuild · Classic · Light mode · Simulated data" />
      <ProductCapture name="live-fx-table.png" alt="Enhanced FX public rebuild with its table view selected" href="https://shannonhecker.github.io/fx-execution-analytics/" label="Public rebuild · Enhanced · Table view · Simulated data" />
    </section>
        <ProductCapture name="live-fx-compare.png" alt="Two simulated FX orders compared side by side in the current public rebuild" href="https://shannonhecker.github.io/fx-execution-analytics/" label="My public rebuild · Compare orders" />
        <FxDesignEvidence />
        <p class="lead muted">Hover a fill to see the detail. Right-click to act. Drag a price line to reprice, and it opens a filled-in order ticket. Each step commits you a little more, so you can't reprice by accident. We turned a chart you watch into a chart you can trade from.</p>
        <Flow
          label="Monitoring to action, step by step"
          steps={[
            { t: 'Hover a fill', d: 'See the detail for that clip' },
            { t: 'Right-click', d: 'Act on the exact time and price under the cursor' },
            { t: 'Drag the limit line', d: 'Reprice where the decision was made' },
            { t: 'Pre-filled ticket', d: 'What changes, before anything does', branch: { t: 'Cancel', d: 'Nothing changes' } },
            { t: 'Confirm', d: 'The compliance step, still on the chart' },
            { t: 'Audit trail', d: 'Every change to the order is recorded' },
          ]}
          caption="Each step commits you a little more. Confirmation and audit never mean leaving the chart."
        />
        <ul class="rows">
          <li><b>Built in six phases</b><p>Theming and data integration first, then display, then interaction, and order submission last. Each phase reduced the risk of the next, and the desk got something usable early.</p></li>
          <li><b>Too much on the axis, by design, for now</b><p>Phase one puts every attribute on screen on purpose, so the desk can tell us what matters instead of us guessing. Phase two adds entitlement-based templates per user type and a preference panel to hide attributes. Start wide, then narrow with evidence.</p></li>
          <li><b>Two version-one debts and the next iteration</b><p>My public rebuild still uses patterned state regions and individual fill dots. The diagrams below show the refinements I designed: a separate state lane and counted clusters that expand on zoom. Patterned regions make the exceptional read as normal, and volume-sized dots become a smear at three hundred fills.</p></li>
          <li><b>A compare view, because quality is relative</b><p>Execution quality only means something against a benchmark or another order, so I put a patient working order beside an urgent completed one, with the benchmark metrics in one table.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <BeforeAfter label="Version-one debt: order states" before="States tiled the canvas" beforeNote="Patterned regions sat behind the prices, so the exceptional read as normal." after="States in their own lane" afterNote="A lane under the time axis. The price field stays quiet.">
          <svg slot="before" viewBox="0 0 320 120" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: hatched state regions behind the price line">
            <defs><pattern id="ba-h" width="8" height="8" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)"><path d="M0 0 V8" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" opacity=".25" /></pattern></defs>
            <rect x="60" y="10" width="70" height="90" fill="url(#ba-h)" /><rect x="178" y="10" width="74" height="90" fill="url(#ba-h)" />
            <path d="M10 70 L50 52 L90 64 L130 38 L170 48 L210 30 L250 44 L310 26" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
            <path d="M10 100 H310" stroke="currentColor" opacity=".4" />
          </svg>
          <svg slot="after" viewBox="0 0 320 120" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: a clean price line with states in a labelled lane under the axis">
            <path d="M10 60 L50 42 L90 54 L130 28 L170 38 L210 20 L250 34 L310 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
            <path d="M10 86 H310" stroke="currentColor" opacity=".4" />
            <rect x="60" y="94" width="70" height="14" rx="2" fill="currentColor" opacity=".85" /><rect x="178" y="94" width="74" height="14" rx="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" />
            <text x="95" y="104.5" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-family="Inter, sans-serif">Suspended</text><text x="215" y="104.5" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor" font-family="Inter, sans-serif">Outside limit</text>
          </svg>
        </BeforeAfter>
        <BeforeAfter label="Version-one debt: fills at scale" before="A dot per fill" beforeNote="Volume-sized dots were honest at twenty clips and a smear at three hundred." after="Counted clusters" afterNote="Fills group into clusters with a count, and expand on zoom.">
          <svg slot="before" viewBox="0 0 320 120" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: hundreds of overlapping fill dots forming a smear">
            <g fill="currentColor" opacity=".35">{Array.from({ length: 160 }, (_, i) => <circle cx={12 + ((i * 37) % 296)} cy={30 + ((i * 53) % 60) + Math.sin(i) * 6} r={2 + (i % 4)} />)}</g>
          </svg>
          <svg slot="after" viewBox="0 0 320 120" role="img" aria-label="Schematic: five counted clusters of fills">
            {[[40, 70, 48], [100, 50, 112], [165, 64, 31], [228, 40, 87], [286, 58, 22]].map(([x, y, n]) => <g><circle cx={x} cy={y} r={10 + n / 10} fill="currentColor" /><text x={x} y={y + 3.5} font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-family="Inter, sans-serif" font-weight="600">{n}</text></g>)}
          </svg>
        </BeforeAfter>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-edge">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>07</b> Edge and validation states</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="The states a trader hits when things are not normal." />
        <Matrix
          caption="Designed edge and validation states. The current public rebuild demonstrates a subset; the state lane and counted clusters are shown as design schematics above."
          cols={['State', 'What the screen does', 'Why']}
          rows={[
            ['Suspended, delayed start, outside limit', 'Each gets its own labelled band in a lane under the time axis.', 'The price field stays quiet, and the state is readable at a glance.'],
            ['Drag to reprice', 'Opens a pre-filled ticket. Nothing changes until it is confirmed.', 'You cannot reprice by accident. Each step commits you a little more.'],
            ['Colour-blind trader, washed-out screen', 'Bid, ask, mid, limit and fills each have their own shape and dash. Favourable and adverse say so in words.', 'Colour never carries meaning alone.'],
            ['Hundreds of fills', 'Fills group into counted clusters that expand on zoom.', 'Honest at twenty clips and at three hundred.'],
            ['A new order, no data yet', 'Empty, then loading, states in place of an empty chart.', 'The screen never looks broken while data arrives.'],
            ['Dark room or bright room', 'Full light and dark themes.', 'Traders run dark rooms and sales run bright ones.'],
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="beat rv" id="fx-outcome">
      <div class="label"><HL class="k"><b>08</b> Outcome</HL></div>
      <div class="body">
        <Split as="h3" class="h-sub" text="Where it is today, and how it will be measured." />
        <div class="metrics">
          <div class="metric"><span class="big" data-count="6">6</span><span>build phases. The spec was accepted by business and vendor as the contract</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><span class="big w">Beta</span><span>in use with internal users at Barclays today</span></div>
          <div class="metric"><span class="big" data-count="3">3</span><span>measures agreed for rollout. Price move to action, tool switches per order, usage per feature</span></div>
        </div>
        <Demo src="/media/fx-current.webm" poster="live-fx.png" width={1600} height={1000} label="Enhanced view, filter controls, comparison and the order ticket. All data is simulated." />
        <ProductCapture name="live-fx-filters.png" alt="The current FX filters for venue, fill side and execution states" href="https://shannonhecker.github.io/fx-execution-analytics/" label="My public rebuild · Filter controls" />
        <p class="muted measure">No adoption numbers are claimed before rollout. I hold my designs to the same standard as the data. What I'd do differently: direct trader access earlier, instead of going through the business first. And the vendor's engine limits discovered in week one, not mid-spec.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ResponsiveEvidence project="fx" />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
.fx-source-layers{margin:40px 0 16px;min-width:0}.fx-source-layers img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:var(--radius)}.fx-source-layers figcaption{font-size:13px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6;margin-top:12px}.fx-layer-transcript{margin:16px 0 48px}.fx-layer-transcript summary{cursor:pointer;padding-block:12px;color:var(--fg);font-size:15px}
</style>
